I think this is good enough for now, but I had expected that the destination service would derive ownership information from Kubernetes, not from labels injected into the pods. This is particularly relevant for things like ReplicaSets, where Conduit has no knowledge of this at deployment inject time, so those labels will never be set by Conduit.

Related, I am re-introducing a `getPodOwner` method into public-api, cribbed from the telemetry service, that will probably do what we need:
https://github.com/runconduit/conduit/blob/813aa0fddcf5a01ea673a7204ebe2da6932ef4a7/controller/api/public/grpc_server.go#L529-L546

```go
func (s *grpcServer) getPodOwner(pod *k8sV1.Pod) (string, error) {
	namespace := pod.Namespace
	if len(pod.GetOwnerReferences()) == 0 {
		return "", fmt.Errorf("Pod %s has no owner", pod.Name)
	}
	parent := pod.GetOwnerReferences()[0]
	if parent.Kind == "ReplicaSet" {
		rs, err := s.replicaSetLister.GetPodReplicaSets(pod)
		if err != nil {
			return "", err
		}
		if len(rs) == 0 || len(rs[0].GetOwnerReferences()) == 0 {
			return namespace + "/" + parent.Name, nil
		}
		return namespace + "/" + rs[0].GetOwnerReferences()[0].Name, nil
	}
	return namespace + "/" + parent.Name, nil
}
```

You call `inner.get(&key)` here because `inner.insert(key, new_value)` consumes `new_value`, right? However, isn't this cure worse than the problem? In particular, `inner.get(&key)` would likely be as slow or slower than cloning `new_value`, right?

I think instead we could instead do something like this, with one lookup:

```rust
inner.entry(&key)
    .and_modify(|old| {
        if *old != new {
            *old = new;
            on_change(....)
        }
    })
    .or_insert(...);
```

Or, the equivalent using pattern matching on the result of `inner.entry(&key)`.

Do you think that using the entry API solves this problem and/or the other related issues?

I think it would better for this method to only return successfully if the owner object is actually a deployment. As written this will return replication controllers, jobs, etc. The new stat API only treats actual deployments as deployments, and since these two APIs work in conjunction together, I'd like for them to be consistent. I think something like this would work:

```
func (s *grpcServer) getDeploymentFor(pod *k8sV1.Pod) (string, error) {
	namespace := pod.Namespace
	if len(pod.GetOwnerReferences()) == 0 {
		return "", fmt.Errorf("Pod %s has no owner", pod.Name)
	}
	parent := pod.GetOwnerReferences()[0]
	if parent.Kind != "ReplicaSet" {
		return "", fmt.Errorf("Pod %s parent is not a ReplicaSet", pod.Name)
	}
	rs, err := s.replicaSetLister.GetPodReplicaSets(pod)
	if err != nil {
		return "", err
	}
	if len(rs) == 0 || len(rs[0].GetOwnerReferences()) == 0 {
		return "", fmt.Errorf("Pod %s has no replicasets", pod.Name)
	}

	for _, r := range rs {
		for _, owner := range r.GetOwnerReferences() {
			switch owner.Kind {
			case "Deployment":
				return namespace + "/" + owner.Name, nil
			}
		}
	}

	return "", fmt.Errorf("Pod %s owner is not a Deployment")
}
```

@briansmith I agree with your concerns; however, it appears that the request is constructed in a library (either in `h2` [here](https://github.com/carllerche/h2/blob/fabae35311362cf24fa9e2e08e27c3d64f6c67b1/src/server.rs#L493) or in `hyper` [here](https://github.com/hyperium/hyper/blob/dfdca25c004aeb583b931f57e19510b3096362e3/src/proto/h1/dispatch.rs#L355-L359), depending on the request protocol). The middleware is the earliest point that _Conduit_ has access to the request struct.

I didn't think that PRs against those libraries in order to add a Conduit-specific extension seemed likely to be accepted, and therefore, forking our library dependencies didn't seem worth what appears to be a fairly small gain in accuracy, as the service is called with the constructed request shortly thereafter. I _did_ consider using the [`serve_modified`](https://tower-rs.github.io/tower-h2/tower_h2/server/struct.Server.html#method.serve_modified) method in `tower-h2` to add the timestamp header to the request earlier in the pipeline, but there's no similar method in `hyper`, so there would be a difference in accuracy between the HTTP/1 and HTTP/2 timestamps. I felt that using a middleware, while less accurate overall, results in more consistent measurements between both protocols.

If you have any ideas I may have overlooked, I'd appreciate your suggestion, and I'd also like to hear what @olix0r thinks, as well.

