review_threads.body:
Super minor, but there's no need for the else clause here since you're returning from the if clause. This would be more commonly written as:

```
	pod, err := s.podForIP(ip)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	if pod == nil {
		log.WithFields(log.Fields{"ip": addr.PublicIPToString(ip)}).Debugln("no pod for IP")
		return make(map[string]string), nil
	}
```

I appreciate that this method thoroughly handles the case where multiple terminating pods exist for a given IP, but I think the likelihood of that happening is very low, as is the risk of returning the wrong pod. Honestly I'd probably write this as:

```
func (s *server) podForIP(ip *public.IPAddress) (*apiv1.Pod, error) {
	ipStr := addr.PublicIPToString(ip)
	objs, err := s.k8sAPI.Pod().Informer().GetIndexer().ByIndex(podIPIndex, ipStr)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}

	if len(objs) == 1 {
		return objs[0].(*apiv1.Pod), nil
	}

	for _, obj := range objs {
		pod := obj.(*apiv1.Pod)
		if pod.Status.Phase == apiv1.PodRunning {
			return pod, nil
		}
	}

	return nil, nil
}
```

Inspecting the pod container termination timestamps feels a bit too involved for your use case, even if it is the most correct solution. That code is also complicated enough that I'd feel much better if were thoroughly tested in `server_test.go`.

@klingerf I did some research and it looks like the precise meaning of the `Pending` pod phase is that _one or more_ of the containers in the pod have yet to start. So, you're correct that a `Pending` pod may have some containers which have started and have made requests.

I think the most correct way to handle pending pods would involve testing if any of their containers have started, but that seems fairly complex. So, I'm going to go with your original suggestion of only returning  a non-running pod if `len(pods) == 1`.

Sorry if this turned into a bit of a 🚲 🏚 ...

I find `time.Duration` way easier to work with than protobuf durations. If this method accepted `time.Duration` as a parameter, then I'd write it as:

```
func formatDuration(d time.Duration) string {
	if d < time.Millisecond {
		return d.Round(time.Microsecond).String()
	}
	if d < time.Second {
		return d.Round(time.Millisecond).String()
	}
	return d.Round(time.Second).String()
}
```

To do that, I'd convert the protobuf duration to a `time.Duration` as soon as it's received (using [this func](https://github.com/golang/protobuf/blob/master/ptypes/duration.go#L76)), and then I'd use `time.Duration` throughout the rest of this file.

One more small go style nit -- for multi-line constructors, we tend to use named fields so that there is less chance of field misalignment. So I'd write this as:

```
			id := topRequestID{
				src:    addr.PublicAddressToString(event.GetSource()),
				dst:    addr.PublicAddressToString(event.GetDestination()),
				stream: ev.RequestInit.GetId().Stream,
			}
```

Furthermore, you're initializing the ID in each branch of the case statement, but the `src` and `dst` fields don't change. So you could save yourself a few lines by doing something like:

```
		id := topRequestID{
			src: addr.PublicAddressToString(event.GetSource()),
			dst: addr.PublicAddressToString(event.GetDestination()),
		}

		switch ev := event.GetHttp().GetEvent().(type) {
		case *pb.TapEvent_Http_RequestInit_:
			id.stream = ev.RequestInit.GetId().Stream
			outstandingRequests[id] = topRequest{
				event:   event,
				reqInit: ev.RequestInit,
			}

		case *pb.TapEvent_Http_ResponseInit_:
			id.stream = ev.ResponseInit.GetId().Stream
			if req, ok := outstandingRequests[id]; ok {
				req.rspInit = ev.ResponseInit
				outstandingRequests[id] = req
			} else {
				log.Warnf("Got ResponseInit for unknown stream: %s", id)
			}

		case *pb.TapEvent_Http_ResponseEnd_:
			id.stream = ev.ResponseEnd.GetId().Stream
			if req, ok := outstandingRequests[id]; ok {
				req.rspEnd = ev.ResponseEnd
				requestCh <- req
			} else {
				log.Warnf("Got ResponseEnd for unknown stream: %s)", id)
			}
		}
```

Since not all fields need to be initialized when the struct is instantiated.

TIOLI you could add a `String()` func to your `topRequestID` struct that will do the string formatting automatically. Something like:

```
func (id topRequestID) String() string {
	return fmt.Sprintf("%s->%s(%d)", id.src, id.dst, id.stream)
}
```

And then here and in the other log statement below, you could just let the id be coerced to a string, which will invoke that function:

```
log.Warnf("Got ResponseInit for unknown stream: %s", id)
```

