Okay, in order to figure out how to proceed regarding `tls_enabled` and `enum TLS`...

In an upcoming change, we will remove the CA (as done on identity/master) and the "identity" service will be part of the default control plane installation. The identity service will need some configuration of its own (possibly from the global config... tbd), but the inject controller needs the following information:

- the "trust domain" -- a string, probably stored in the global config
- the "trust anchors" -- a blob of certificates; probably should be stored in a dedicated configmap so changes to this can be audited/acled separately from the base linkerd config.

There will also have to be an annotation that causes the proxy to opt-out of identity, but this probably _only_ needs to be an annotation on the target pods. We can support namespace/parent overrides, as well...

So, with this in mind, I expect us to end up with _at least_ something like the following...

```proto3
message GlobalConfig {
...
  IdentityContext identity_context = NN;
}

message IdentityContext {
  string trust_domain = 1;
}
```

So, if we want to introduce this message type now (maybe without the `trust_domain` until we need it), we could use the default null value to indicate what is currently "tls=disabled" and use it as set as "tls=optional" -- this is obviously a hack, but this should be very temporary (and documented).

Alternatively, if this is too subtle, we may want to just have a tls_enabled boolean that we put on a high-numbered field that we'll immediately deprecate...

review_threads.body:
My vote is to start small here and only include the global config options that are required for implementing injection. I believe that would be:

```
message GlobalConfig {
  string linkerd_namespace = 1;
  string linkerd_version = 2;
  TLS tls = 3; // (or maybe bool tls_enabled?)
  bool cni_enabled = 4;
}
```

Additional notes about this:

* We don't need the `api_addr` configuration option, since it only applies as an override for the CLI to connect to the public API -- I wouldn't expect any of the control plane components to read the value of that field

* I do think we should include the TLS configuration option here, instead of in the `ProxyConfig` struct, since many control plane components will need to know TLS settings (maybe @olix0r can weigh in on best way to represent this)

* Instead of `no_init_container`, let's call that `cni_enabled`, since that's more reflective of the overall configuration of the control plane, and bypassing the init container is just an implementation detail

* I'm torn about the `auto_inject` configuration option. It feels like that could also be a flag that's passed into the proxy-injector process at startup. My hesitation is that I don't know how the behavior of the proxy-injector would change if the value of that field is modified. Would we watch the value and then delete the MWC? Until we implement something like that, it feels like auto-injection can only be enabled/disabled at install time, in which case we can just pass it in as a flag.

* We can add additional global configuration fields, such as `cluster_domain`, as we implement them later

