I think this logic can be simplified a bit. Currently, we're building an array of searchable text, and then running another loop to check whether matching text exists in each entry in textToSearch and then putting it in filteredRows if there's a match.

If we use `.filter` I think we can shorten some of this logic. All we really want to do is, for each row, go through all the columns **until** we find any column that matches the filter text (additionally, we don't need to check the rest of the columns, we already know that /something/ in the row matches).

The snippet below uses `.filter` so that we don't have to build up an array ourselves, and uses `.some` to keep executing `col.filter` on the filterable columns, stopping once any column for that row has some kind of match. (This could have some savings if we had a lot of filterable columns)

```
    if (filterBy) {
      let columnsToFilter = tableColumns.filter(col => col.filter);
      let filteredRows = tableRows.filter(row => {
        return columnsToFilter.some(col => {
          let rowText = col.filter(row);
          return rowText.match(filterBy);
        });
      });

      rows = filteredRows;
    }
```

