I think you misunderstood. I'll explain the problem in detail now from the start. I do understand that we still want to keep `if err != nil`.

Consider this snippet

```golang
resultChan := make(chan metricsResult, len(pods))
var activeRoutines int32
for _, pod := range pods {
	containers, err := getAllContainersWithPort(pod, portName)
	if err != nil {
		resultChan <- metricsResult{
			pod: pod.GetName(),
			err: err,
		}
		continue
	}

	for i := range containers {
		atomic.AddInt32(&activeRoutines, 1)
		go func(p corev1.Pod, c corev1.Container) {
			defer atomic.AddInt32(&activeRoutines, -1)
			// get metrics and send to channel
		}(pod, containers[i])
	}
}

for {
	select {
	case result := <-resultChan:
		results = append(results, result)
	case <-time.After(waitingTime):
		break // timed out
	}
	if atomic.LoadInt32(&activeRoutines) == 0 {
		break
	}
}
```

Imagine if there was an error in getting the containers for all of the pods i.e. the `getAllContainersWithPort` method returns an error for each pod of the `pods` slice. This would mean that the `activeRoutines` variable stays set to 0. Now consider the `for` loop where we check if the `activeRoutines` variable is 0 or not.
In this particular case, the `if` condition will be satisfied and the loop will break just after getting one entry from the `resultsChan`.

