@adleong Heh, well, you know me.. I'd rather hide those other flags.

My way of thinking is that `install`/`upgrade` are meant to be simple interfaces for getting started, but more serious customization should be done by helm etc...

My main concern is that we have a pretty tight coupling between the install/upgrade command and the control planes they install. Very bad  things can happen if people try to use upgrade with a specified control plane version (i.e. without the newer version of the linkerd CLI). So I'd really rather not even open the possibility for having to answer those questions in Slack ;p

I believe that liveness checks should fail if the trust roots don't match, but it may be difficult to get a helpful error message.  From the local proxy's point of view, it will just see this as a connection refused.  

Requiring that the trust roots match exactly is a bit naive but requiring this simplifies things.  Strictly speaking, we only need the remote's issuer certificate to validate against our trust root and vice versa.  But I don't think we have a good way of reading the issuer certificate without having access to the issuer secret.

@grampelberg makes a good point about the check being "point-in-time".  If the multi-cluster service account has permission to read configmaps in the control plane namespace, then the check could use the remote credentials to fetch the remote trust root when the check is run and compare it to the local trust root.

Very good feedback! So... 

> Thinking about edge cases, would cross-cluster communication work if they had different anchors that were themselves rooted on the same root cert? Or what if one if the clusters had an anchor with a bundle containing the other cluster's cert? If so, can this check be more generic to allow those situations?

@alpeb So in this case, I think we can be a bit less naive and actually try to verify the current issuer cert (we grab it from the identity issuer) of the local cluster with the roots of the remote cluster. Since the proxy certs are issued off of the issuer cert, that should cover all cases that you mention, right ? 

@grampelberg @adleong The point in time situation you are describing is correct. So this clearly has its limitations. Granting permissions to look-up the remote cluster's linkerd config map is fine with me. That was my original idea but decided to not be so relaxed with RBACs and opt for the current approach (clearly I did not think through the edge cases). The only complication I see here is that... the local cluster would have to know the namespace in which Linkerd is installed on the remote cluster. How can we do that. Is annotation on the credentials secret a good enough approach? Clearly we would have to assume that this  Linkerd installation does not move namespaces... 

One last thing.. 

> I believe that liveness checks should fail if the trust roots don't match, but it may be difficult to get a helpful error message. From the local proxy's point of view, it will just see this as a connection refused.

@adleong As a matter of fact the service mirroring controller, which is the same component performing the checks is not injected with the proxy by default. It is a separate chart, installed with a separate command and by default is running without the proxy. So the checks would not fail.. Do we want it to be injected? Is that a requirement? WDYT?

> Can you help me think through the alternatives? Can we know the node's IP at pod-creation time and allow traffic that originates from the node?

@olix0r Possibly, but as far as I take it,  pods can move nodes. I also feel it is not the best way to do all that but could not think of a much better way. Alternatively we can rely on something that is NOT an http get request at all for checking health locally. We can configure the healthcheks to actually execute a shell command on the container and check whether the nginx process is running. Now that I think about it.. this sounds like a better approach. 

This is a nice example of what I am talking about: https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/#define-a-liveness-command

@alpeb As you know we can't really put the defaults into `charts/linkerd2/values.yaml` as that would mean that the defaults would go into the `linkerd-config-addons`, causing upgrades to not work as expected.

Putting these values in templates might lead into conflicts when a user tries to override the same field that was set with a default value, as we do not have a way to merge them (with values in templates) but we just append them and prometheus fails starting up telling that the same value has been set twice and has conflicts.

By having these defaults in `/<add-on>/values.yaml`, we just merge them with the overriden values thus the same field will be overriden but not appended, causing it to work as expected.

