Hm, that's interesting. Since I have started working on EndpointSlices, ports have been a major headache to deal with and there's quite a bit of confusion around it from my side.

The way I have been thinking about this has been based on the documentation on Endpoints in client-go. Specifically:
```
// EndpointSubset is a group of addresses with a common set of ports. The
// expanded set of endpoints is the Cartesian product of Addresses x Ports.
// For example, given:
//   {
//     Addresses: [{"ip": "10.10.1.1"}, {"ip": "10.10.2.2"}],
//     Ports:     [{"name": "a", "port": 8675}, {"name": "b", "port": 309}]
//   }
// The resulting set of endpoints can be viewed as:
//     a: [ 10.10.1.1:8675, 10.10.2.2:8675 ],
//     b: [ 10.10.1.1:309, 10.10.2.2:309 ]
```

They say an Endpoints object is the union of all subsets, where a subset is defined as a group of addresses with common ports. By this logic, I assumed an EndpointSlice is simply a subset -- a group of addresses (within a given limit, default 100 of them) that use the same ports. I have rarely encountered any endpoints with more than a subset, however, and replicating it has been a bit difficult, so I never saw my assumptions play out in minikube.

The only mention of subsets in EndpointSlices is:
```
// EndpointSlice represents a subset of the endpoints that implement a service.
// For a given service there may be multiple EndpointSlice objects, selected by
// labels, which must be joined to produce the full set of endpoints.
```

What do you think? Is there any easy way to test our assumptions or am I missing something very obvious?
Also, just to confirm: my assumption all along has been that EndpointSlices are sliced based on the common ports they share, so different slices would hold addresses that match that respective slice's port.

So from the comments of the proxy api: 

```
// If the service does not exist then the controller must send
// `no_endpoints{exists: false}` ASAP when a client subscribes or when the
// service stops existing. If the service exists and has endpoints available
// then the controller must send `add` that lists all (or at least a large
// number) of the endpoints for the service. If and only if the service exists
// but does not have any endpoints available then the controller SHOULD send
// `no_endpoints{exists: true}` when a client subscribes. In other words, the
// `no_endpoints` message must only be sent when there are *no*endpoints for
// the service.
```

What happens if this is nil? The docs say something intersting that I am not sure I can interpret correctly: 

```
	// ports specifies the list of network ports exposed by each endpoint in
	// this slice. Each port must have a unique name. When ports is empty, it
	// indicates that there are no defined ports. When a port is defined with a
	// nil port value, it indicates "all ports". Each slice may include a
	// maximum of 100 ports.
	// +optional
	// +listType=atomic
```

All ports?... not sure what they mean here. Any idea?

